    Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;179;-720,1072;Inherit;False;246.3002;247.05;View
    Vector WS;1;178;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode, AmplifyShaderEditor,
    Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;126;-704,1360;Inherit;False;786.7621;551.8051;View
    Vector World Space;6;85;87;86;103;94;88;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;65;-768,-416;Inherit;False;619;445;Pointing
    toward the Camera View;5;66;64;62;63;61;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;56;-768,-976;Inherit;False;1542.177;484.8575;View
    Direction;10;169;168;167;166;92;57;59;60;55;58;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;71;-768,96;Inherit;False;1331.987;605.7159;
    Fresnel Effect;11;81;82;80;79;78;77;76;75;74;73;72;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;132;1152,2384;Inherit;False;205.5;272.5;Mask
    Surface;1;154;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode, AmplifyShaderEditor,
    Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;133;-720,2624;Inherit;False;716.191;380.8127;NdotV;5;151;150;149;148;147;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;134;1152,2672;Inherit;False;203.5;271;Mask;1;152;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;135;1152,2960;Inherit;False;203.5;271;Mask
    Edge;1;153;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.CommentaryNode, AmplifyShaderEditor,
    Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;182;624,1728;Inherit;False;138.5;124.5;View
    Vector;1;183;;0,0,0,1;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode, AmplifyShaderEditor,
    Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;57;-720,-912;Inherit;False;498.4548;189.3724;;;0,0,0,1;The
    View Dir node outputs the current camera view direction on a coordinates space
    defined by the Space parameter.$$World Space coordinates will be the most commonly
    used option but Tangent Space is also very helpful mostly with operations over
    normal maps ( which are usually in tangent space ).;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;59;512,-896;Inherit;False;223.3999;113;Space
    World;;0,0,0,1;Coordinate space based on absolute world coordinates;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;60;512,-704;Inherit;False;224.0127;109.3468;Space
    Tangent;;0,0,0,1;Coordinate space based on a surface tangent plane.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;66;-496,-144;Inherit;False;288.7054;138.1758;;;0,0,0,1;By
    doing a dot product between the View Direction and the Normal Vector, you can
    create a mask that's white when the surface is facing the camera and black when
    it's facing away.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode, AmplifyShaderEditor,
    Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;72;112,368;Inherit;False;255.9122;100;;;0,0,0,1;Raising
    the result to a power controls the falloff curve.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;73;-496,384;Inherit;False;244.1442;130.8911;;;0.03773582,0.03773582,0.03773582,1;The
    dot product is white where the normal and view vector are parallel and black
    where they're perpendicular.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode, AmplifyShaderEditor,
    Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;74;-144,368;Inherit;False;236.7892;117.652;;;0,0,0,1;Most
    use-cases call for the mask to be inverted so we do that with the One Minus node.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;75;-144,496;Inherit;False;526.1356;184.8061;Fresnel
    Effect Node;;0,0,0,1;Creates a mask based on the relationship between the view
    direction and the surface normal.  When the mesh's surface is facing the camera,
    the mask is black.  When it's facing away, the mask is white.$$The Power input
    can be used to control the falloff curve of the effect. Values higher than 1
    push the black toward the glancing angles. Values lower than 1 pull the white
    toward the camera-facing angles.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;88;-656,1664;Inherit;False;249.4012;143.1976;;;0,0,0,1;You
    can create the same vector as the View Dir node by subtracting the Position in
    World Space from the Camera Position. $;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;91;-464,800;Inherit;False;541.4526;250.555;;;0.05660379,0.05660379,0.05660379,1;The
    difference between the View Vector and the View Direction:$$View Direction:$The
    View Direction is a normalized vector (so it's only 1 unit long). It's best used
    to find the ANGLE of the camera and other vectors.$$View Vector:$The View Vector
    is a line that extends all the way from the current fragment or vertex to the
    camera - so it can be used to get the DISTANCE to the camera.$$Both vectors point
    in the same direction.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode, AmplifyShaderEditor,
    Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;92;-720,-704;Inherit;False;354.1746;102.5911;Safe
    Normalize;;0,0,0,1;Makes sure view vector is not zero, even when scene has no
    cameras;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode, AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0,
    Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;94;-448,1408;Inherit;False;484.6892;100;World
    Space Camera Pos Node;;0,0,0,1;The World Space Camera Pos node outputs the current
    camera position in world space.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;95;-464,1072;Inherit;False;543.5508;246.4944;View
    Vector;;0,0,0,1;Creates a vector from the camera to the object currently being
    rendered $$- unique for each object.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;103;-160,1744;Inherit;False;199.9;103;;;0,0,0,1;Distance
    between the camera and the current position.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;111;800,1488;Inherit;False;305.9239;129.7988;;;0,0,0,1;By
    measuring the length of the View Vector, you can find out how far each pixel
    is from the camera. The resulting distance value is in meters.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;112;784,1856;Inherit;False;301.7797;117.0062;;;0,0,0,1;By
    measuring the length of the View Vector, you can find out how far each pixel
    is from the camera. The resulting distance value is in meters.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.StickyNoteNode,
    AmplifyShaderEditor, Version=0.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null;116;1664,2000;Inherit;False;285;134;;;0,0,0,1;This
    type of mask can be used to fade out close-up details when the camera is futher
    away or make other visual changes based on camera distance.;0;0\nNode;AmplifyShaderEditor.OneMinusNode,
